#N canvas 100 100 700 640 10;
#X obj 40 40 jet_win;
#X msg 40 100 create;
#X msg 130 100 destroy;
#X obj 40 150 jet_head 0;
#X obj 40 180 jet_rotateXYZ 0 0 0;
#X obj 40 210 jet_translateXYZ 0 0 0;
#X obj 40 240 jet_scaleXYZ 1 1 1;
#X obj 40 270 jet_rectangle -2 0 1.2 1.2 1 0.6 0.3;
#X obj 200 270 jet_cube 2 0.8 0.5 0.3;
#X msg 350 150 set 0 0 45;
#X msg 460 150 set 45 0 0;
#X msg 570 150 set 0 45 0;
#X msg 350 180 set 0 0 0;
#X text 350 200 rotateXYZ: Z (both react) \, X \, Y (cube only) \, reset;
#X msg 350 240 set 1 1 0;
#X msg 460 240 set 0 0 0;
#X text 350 260 translateXYZ: shift \, reset;
#X msg 350 290 set 1.6 1.6 1.6;
#X msg 460 290 set 1 1 1;
#X text 350 310 scaleXYZ: bigger \, reset;
#X text 40 320 jet_rotateXYZ/translateXYZ/scaleXYZ (like Gem's
own) each update BOTH transforms every bang - the 2D 3x3 one (jet_triangle/jet_rectangle
- the square on the left here) and the 3D 4x4 one (jet_cube - on
the right). the SAME chain drives both\, but they don't see the
same thing out of it: 2D only has meaning in a flat plane\, so it
only ever picks up the Z rotation and the X/Y translate/scale - X/Y
rotation and Z translate/scale are 3D-only and the square simply
ignores them. try "set 45 0 0" or "set 0 45 0" on rotateXYZ - only
the cube spins \; then "set 0 0 45" - now the square rotates too.
every jet_head dispatch resets both transforms to identity first
\, so this chain's accumulated state never leaks into another chain
\, and doesn't carry over between bangs either - that's why every
message here is "set the value \, then bang the chain" rather than
something that keeps accumulating on repeated bangs. jet_head only
bangs automatically while jet_win is rendering (after "create")
- these test messages update the stored values right away regardless
\, but you won't see the change until the next render tick bangs
the chain.;
#X floatatom 700 150 5 0 0 0 - - -;
#X floatatom 700 190 5 0 0 0 - - -;
#X text 660 130 X (hot);
#X text 660 170 Z (hot too);
#X text 40 460 all three transform objects also have 3 Gem-style
inlets now - X/Y/Z are ALL hot (like real Gem's rotateXYZ/translateXYZ/scaleXYZ
\, unlike the more common Pd convention of only the leftmost inlet
being hot). a float to any one of them immediately recomputes and
forwards \, like a bang \, using whatever the OTHER two axes are
currently stored as. drag either number box above - the cube reacts
right away on every drag \, from either one.;
#X connect 1 0 0 0;
#X connect 2 0 0 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 6 0 8 0;
#X connect 9 0 4 0;
#X connect 10 0 4 0;
#X connect 11 0 4 0;
#X connect 12 0 4 0;
#X connect 14 0 5 0;
#X connect 15 0 5 0;
#X connect 17 0 6 0;
#X connect 18 0 6 0;
#X connect 21 0 4 0;
#X connect 22 0 4 2;
